<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hanseatic League</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hanseatic League was a commercial and defensive association of merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> and towns in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, active from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> into the early modern era. Led by cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, it dominated trade in the Baltic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Its cities developed economies largely independent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>'s central authority.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league.jpg" alt="Hanseatic League" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Droysen/Andrée · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ausbreitung_der_Hanse_um_das_Jahr_1400-Droysens_28.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
